{"id":"df01","title":"The Greenhouse Effect","text":"The Earth's climate is {1} by the balance between incoming solar energy and outgoing heat. Gases such as carbon dioxide trap some of this heat, a natural process known as the greenhouse effect. Without it, the average surface temperature would be about minus eighteen degrees Celsius. Since the industrial era, human activity has {2} the concentration of these gases, leading to a gradual {3} in global temperatures. Scientists use computer models to {4} future changes, although these models contain some {5} because the climate system is so complex. Reducing emissions is widely seen as the most {6} way to limit further warming.","words":["uncertainty","ignore","predict","governed","decline","increased","effective","rise"],"answers":["governed","increased","rise","predict","uncertainty","effective"]},
{"id":"df02","title":"Bees and Pollination","text":"Bees play a vital role in agriculture because they {1} pollen from one flower to another, allowing plants to produce fruit and seeds. About three-quarters of the world's leading food crops {2} to some extent on animal pollination. In recent decades, however, populations of wild bees have {3} in many regions. Researchers blame a combination of factors, including the loss of natural habitat, the use of certain pesticides and the spread of {4}. Farmers can help by planting strips of wildflowers beside their fields, which provide food and shelter {5} the insects throughout the year. Protecting pollinators is therefore both an environmental and an {6} priority.","words":["flourished","disease","economy","for","transfer","depend","economic","declined"],"answers":["transfer","depend","declined","disease","for","economic"]},
{"id":"df03","title":"The Renaissance","text":"The Renaissance, meaning rebirth, was a period of renewed interest in classical art and learning that began in Italy in the fourteenth century. Wealthy families such as the Medici of Florence acted as {1} of artists, paying them to produce paintings, sculptures and buildings. Artists developed linear {2}, a technique that allowed them to represent three-dimensional space realistically on a flat surface. Scholars {3} ancient Greek and Latin texts, and the invention of printing helped these works to circulate widely. Leonardo da Vinci is often described as a true Renaissance man because his {4} ranged from painting to engineering and anatomy. The movement gradually {5} northward and influenced the culture of much of {6}.","words":["Europe","rejected","interests","patrons","spread","invaders","rediscovered","perspective"],"answers":["patrons","perspective","rediscovered","interests","spread","Europe"]},
{"id":"df04","title":"The Stock Market","text":"A stock market is a place where shares in companies are bought and sold. When a company wants to raise money, it can {1} shares to the public, and in return investors become partial owners. The price of a share reflects what buyers are willing to pay, which changes according to company {2}, interest rates and general confidence. Prices can fall sharply if investors become {3} about the future, as happened in the crash of 1929. Because markets are {4}, even experts find it hard to predict short-term movements. Most financial advisers therefore recommend that people {5} their savings across different types of investment in order to reduce {6}.","words":["borrow","risk","confident","spread","anxious","performance","issue","unpredictable"],"answers":["issue","performance","anxious","unpredictable","spread","risk"]},
{"id":"df05","title":"Sign Languages","text":"Sign languages are fully developed natural languages that use hand shapes, facial expressions and body movement instead of sound. They are not simply gestures {1} spoken language; each has its own grammar and vocabulary. British Sign Language and American Sign Language, for example, are {2} different even though English is spoken in both countries. Research on deaf children has shown that when they are {3} to sign from an early age, they acquire language at a similar pace to hearing children. Brain imaging studies suggest that signers use many of the same regions as speakers when they {4} language. In 2003 the UK government formally {5} British Sign Language as a language in its own right, a step that campaigners had long {6}.","words":["forbidden","process","demanded","reducing","completely","recognised","exposed","representing"],"answers":["representing","completely","exposed","process","recognised","demanded"]},
{"id":"df06","title":"Types of Motivation","text":"Psychologists distinguish between intrinsic and extrinsic motivation. Intrinsic motivation comes from {1} the task itself, such as reading a novel because you find it fascinating. Extrinsic motivation, by contrast, depends on external {2}, such as money, grades or praise. Although rewards can encourage people in the short term, studies suggest that offering payment for an activity people already enjoy may {3} their interest in it. Teachers and managers are therefore advised to give feedback that supports a sense of {4}, meaning the feeling of being in control of one's own actions. When people feel competent and {5} to their group, they are more likely to persist when tasks become {6}.","words":["automatic","connected","difficult","increase","autonomy","enjoying","reduce","rewards"],"answers":["enjoying","rewards","reduce","autonomy","connected","difficult"]},
{"id":"df07","title":"The Rise of Skyscrapers","text":"The first skyscrapers appeared in Chicago in the 1880s, made possible by two innovations: the steel frame and the safety elevator. Earlier buildings relied on thick masonry walls to {1} their weight, which limited how tall they could rise. A steel frame, in contrast, carries the load, so the outer walls can be thin and filled with large windows. The inventor Elisha Otis demonstrated his safety brake in 1854, {2} passengers that the cable could not send them plunging to the ground. Today, engineers design tall buildings to {3} strong winds, sometimes by installing a heavy pendulum near the top that {4} the sway. The Burj Khalifa in Dubai, for many years the world's tallest building, reaches exactly 828 metres and was completed in {5}. Their height is now limited less by engineering than by {6}.","words":["alarming","2010","reassuring","cost","withstand","support","reduces","1910"],"answers":["support","reassuring","withstand","reduces","2010","cost"]},
{"id":"df08","title":"Dietary Fibre","text":"Dietary fibre is the part of plant foods that the body cannot {1}. Although it provides no energy, it plays an important role in health. Soluble fibre, found in oats and beans, can help to {2} cholesterol levels, while insoluble fibre, found in whole grains, keeps food moving through the {3} system. Studies link diets rich in fibre to a lower {4} of type 2 diabetes and heart disease. Despite these benefits, many people in wealthy countries eat less than the {5} amount, partly because processed foods have had much of their fibre removed. Nutritionists suggest replacing white bread with wholegrain {6}.","words":["recommended","produce","digestion","risk","alternatives","digest","lower","digestive"],"answers":["digest","lower","digestive","risk","recommended","alternatives"]},
{"id":"df09","title":"Space Telescopes","text":"The Hubble Space Telescope was launched in 1990 and orbits the Earth at an altitude of about 540 kilometres. Because it is above the atmosphere, which distorts and {1} some light, it can capture images of exceptional clarity. Shortly after launch, astronomers discovered that the main mirror had been ground to the wrong shape, causing {2} pictures. In 1993 astronauts installed corrective optics, and the telescope has since {3} to transform our understanding of the universe. Its observations of distant galaxies have helped astronomers to {4} how galaxies formed and evolved. Hubble's successor, the James Webb Space Telescope, launched in 2021, observes mainly in infrared {5}, and the two instruments are {6} rather than competing.","words":["sharp","complementary","blurred","brightens","wavelengths","understand","absorbs","continued"],"answers":["absorbs","blurred","continued","understand","wavelengths","complementary"]},
{"id":"df10","title":"The Antarctic Treaty","text":"The Antarctic Treaty was signed in 1959 by twelve countries and entered into {1} in 1961. It declares that the continent shall be used for peaceful purposes only and bans military activity and nuclear {2}. Scientific research is encouraged, and researchers from different nations regularly share their {3} and facilities. The treaty also freezes existing territorial claims, so that no country can {4} new ones while it remains in force. A later agreement, the Madrid Protocol of 1991, designated Antarctica as a natural reserve devoted to peace and science and prohibited commercial mining. Many observers regard the system as a rare example of international {5} that has {6} for more than sixty years.","words":["abandoned","claim","cooperation","conflict","force","explosions","endured","data"],"answers":["force","explosions","data","claim","cooperation","endured"]},
{"id":"df11","title":"Bird Migration","text":"Every year, billions of birds travel thousands of kilometres between their breeding and wintering grounds. The Arctic tern holds the record for the longest migration, flying from the Arctic to the Antarctic and {1}, a round trip of up to 70,000 kilometres. Birds use several cues to {2} their way, including the position of the sun, the stars and the Earth's magnetic field. Young birds of some species make their first journey alone, which suggests that the route is partly {3} rather than learned. Migration is dangerous, and many birds die from exhaustion or from {4} with buildings. Climate change is also altering the timing of journeys, so that some species now arrive after the insects they feed on have already {5}, which {6} their chances of raising chicks.","words":["collisions","improves","peaked","ignored","back","inherited","reduces","find"],"answers":["back","find","inherited","collisions","peaked","reduces"]},
{"id":"df12","title":"Learning a First Language","text":"Children acquire their first language with remarkable speed. Most babies begin to babble at around six months, and their first words usually {1} between ten and fourteen months. By the age of two, many children combine words into simple sentences, and by five they have mastered most of the basic {2} of their language. Linguists disagree about how this happens. Some argue that children are born with an inborn {3} for grammar, while others emphasise the role of interaction with caregivers. Evidence for the importance of early input comes from rare cases of children who were {4} from human contact and later found it extremely difficult to learn to speak. Today, most researchers accept that both biology and experience {5} to language development, although they continue to debate the {6} of each.","words":["vanish","isolated","rhythm","importance","appear","capacity","rules","contribute"],"answers":["appear","rules","capacity","isolated","contribute","importance"]},
{"id":"df13","title":"How Insurance Works","text":"Insurance is a way of sharing risk. People who buy a policy pay a regular fee, called a premium, to an insurance company. If a covered event such as a fire or a car accident occurs, the company {1} the policyholder for the loss. The system works because only a small {2} of customers claim in any given year, so the premiums of the many pay for the losses of the few. Insurers employ specialists called actuaries to {3} the probability of different events using statistics. Young drivers pay more because they are statistically more likely to be involved in {4}. A problem called moral hazard arises when people take greater risks because they know they are {5}. To discourage this, policies often require customers to {6} part of the cost themselves.","words":["cancels","bear","proportion","accidents","calculate","protected","compensates","ignore"],"answers":["compensates","proportion","calculate","accidents","protected","bear"]},
{"id":"df14","title":"Electric Vehicles","text":"Electric vehicles use a battery and an electric motor instead of a petrol engine. They produce no exhaust emissions while driving, which helps to improve air quality in {1} areas. However, their overall environmental benefit depends on how the electricity is {2}: a car charged with power from coal causes more emissions than one charged with wind energy. Manufacturing the battery also requires {3} such as lithium, cobalt and nickel, and mining these materials can harm local communities and ecosystems. Even so, studies suggest that over its whole lifetime an electric car usually {4} fewer greenhouse gases than a petrol one. Governments in many countries offer {5} to buyers, and several have announced plans to phase out the sale of new petrol cars in the {6}.","words":["generated","fines","urban","consumes","future","minerals","subsidies","emits"],"answers":["urban","generated","minerals","emits","subsidies","future"]},
{"id":"df15","title":"Ancient Egypt","text":"Ancient Egyptian civilisation developed along the banks of the Nile, whose annual flood left behind rich black soil that made farming {1}. This agricultural surplus supported a large population and allowed some people to {2} in crafts, writing and administration. The Egyptians developed a writing system known as hieroglyphs, which remained a {3} until 1822, when Jean-Francois Champollion deciphered it with the help of the Rosetta Stone. The stone carried the same decree in three scripts, and Champollion could {4} the unknown Egyptian signs with the known Greek text. Egyptian rulers, called pharaohs, were believed to be divine, and enormous tombs such as the pyramids at Giza were built to {5} their bodies and possessions for the afterlife. Today the monuments attract millions of {6} each year.","words":["tourists","destroy","mystery","impossible","specialise","compare","possible","preserve"],"answers":["possible","specialise","mystery","compare","preserve","tourists"]},
{"id":"df16","title":"Deforestation","text":"Forests cover about thirty per cent of the world's land surface, yet every year millions of hectares are {1} for farming, timber and roads. The loss of trees has several consequences. Forests store large quantities of carbon, so clearing them {2} carbon dioxide into the atmosphere. Tree roots also hold soil together, and without them heavy rain can {3} the land, washing fertile earth into rivers. Furthermore, forests are home to a large share of the world's land-based species, so deforestation is a major cause of biodiversity {4}. Some countries have reduced the rate of forest loss through stricter laws and by {5} landowners who protect their trees. Others focus on replanting, although a newly planted forest takes decades to {6} the richness of an original one.","words":["gain","match","erode","absorbs","rewarding","cleared","loss","releases"],"answers":["cleared","releases","erode","loss","rewarding","match"]},
{"id":"df17","title":"Cybersecurity","text":"Cybersecurity is the practice of protecting computers, networks and data from unauthorised access. One of the most common threats is phishing, in which criminals send emails that {1} to come from a trusted organisation in order to trick people into revealing passwords. Another is ransomware, a type of software that locks a victim's files and demands payment before {2} them. Experts advise users to create long, unique passwords and to enable two-factor authentication, which {3} a second proof of identity, such as a code sent to a phone. Organisations should also keep software up to date, because attackers often exploit known {4} that have not yet been repaired. Because human error is involved in many incidents, regular {5} for staff is considered just as important as technical {6}.","words":["rewards","weaknesses","appear","removes","restoring","defences","training","requires"],"answers":["appear","restoring","requires","weaknesses","training","defences"]},
{"id":"df18","title":"The Cure for Scurvy","text":"For centuries, scurvy killed sailors on long voyages. The disease, caused by a lack of vitamin C, produces weakness, bleeding gums and slow healing of wounds. In 1747, the Scottish naval surgeon James Lind carried out one of the first {1} clinical trials, giving different treatments to groups of sailors with the illness. Those who were given oranges and lemons {2} quickly, while the others did not. Although the result was clear, the navy did not {3} citrus juice as standard until nearly fifty years later, and the cure was not fully {4} until vitamin C was identified in the 1930s. The story is often cited as an example of how slowly new ideas can be {5} even when the evidence is strong. Today, scurvy is rare but can still {6} in people with extremely limited diets.","words":["suffered","occur","controlled","accepted","forbid","explained","adopt","recovered"],"answers":["controlled","recovered","adopt","explained","accepted","occur"]}
